624,59,60,641,2790,11,1246,1657,2750,646,387,12180,504,279,7493,400,17,59,50853,220,18,59,50853,220,19,1124,50853,220,20,488,220,16,3,553,38298,73975,30,320,9112,429,55327,8595,3793,374,537,5420,11,1172,38298,73975,568,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe expression $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot 4\\cdot 5+1$ is equal to 121, since multiplication is carried out before addition. However, we can obtain values other than 121 for this expression if we are allowed to change it by inserting parentheses. For example, we can obtain 144 by writing \\[\n(2\\cdot (3\\cdot 4)) \\cdot (5+1) = 144.\n\\]In total, how many values can be obtained from the expression $2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1$ by inserting parentheses? (Note that rearranging terms is not allowed, only inserting parentheses).","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e3e411f191ed33019b827373a37aa154c297b9ac20a6dfb4d781b7c158245649"},"seconds":46.500325441360474,"seed":187925832417371682,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to consider all possible ways to insert parentheses into the expression $2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1$. We will evaluate each case to find the distinct values that can be obtained.\n\nFirst, let's list all possible ways to insert parentheses, keeping the order of the numbers and operations the same:\n\n1. **No parentheses**: \n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\n   \\]\n\n2. **One pair of parentheses around the addition**:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot (5 + 1) = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 = 144\n   \\]\n\n3. **One pair of parentheses around the first two multiplications**:\n   \\[\n   (2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 6 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\n   \\]\n   This is the same as the first case.\n\n4. **One pair of parentheses around the last two multiplications**:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5) + 1 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 20 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\n   \\]\n   This is the same as the first case.\n\n5. **One pair of parentheses around the middle two multiplications**:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1 = 2 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\n   \\]\n   This is the same as the first case.\n\n6. **Two pairs of parentheses**:\n   - Around the first two multiplications and the addition:\n     \\[\n     ((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4) \\cdot (5 + 1) = 6 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 = 144\n     \\]\n     This is the same as the second case.\n   - Around the last two multiplications and the addition:\n     \\[\n     (2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot ((4 \\cdot 5) + 1) = 6 \\cdot 21 = 126\n     \\]\n   - Around the middle two multiplications and the addition:\n     \\[\n     2 \\cdot ((3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot (5 + 1)) = 2 \\cdot (12 \\cdot 6) = 144\n     \\]\n     This is the same as the second case.\n\n7. **Three pairs of parentheses**:\n   - Around all multiplications and the addition:\n     \\[\n     ((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (4 \\cdot (5 + 1))) = (6 \\cdot (4 \\cdot 6)) = 6 \\cdot 24 = 144\n     \\]\n     This is the same as the second case.\n\nFrom the above evaluations, we see that the distinct values obtained are 121, 144, and 126. Therefore, there are 3 distinct values.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
